# EVERY tracked file gets an explicit answer here, because "unspecified"
# means "ask the machine" — and this machine has core.autocrlf=true. Until
# #118 this file named five patterns and the other ~940 tracked files fell
# through to that setting, so they were checked out CRLF while Godot's
# importer rewrites the ones it owns (`*.import`, `*.tres`, `*.tscn`,
# `*.uid`) with LF. The working tree then differed from what was checked
# out, which `git status` reports as ~140 modified files while `git diff`
# shows nothing at all — enough to make `git rebase` refuse to start
# ("cannot rebase: You have unstaged changes") and `git add -A` sweep the
# noise into every commit.
#
# This line is FIRST on purpose. gitattributes is last-match-wins, so
# every rule below overrides it; moving it to the bottom would silently
# take `binary` off every .glb in the repo.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Line endings.
#
# The dev machine has core.autocrlf=true, so without these rules a fresh
# Windows clone checks shell scripts out with CRLF. A CRLF in a shebang
# makes the Linux kernel look for an interpreter literally named
# "/bin/sh\r" and fail with "no such file or directory" — an error that
# points at the file rather than at the line ending, and so costs an hour.
#
# This repo promises that a fresh clone works (./bootstrap.ps1 -> just ->
# test-unit), and gui_entrypoint.sh is COPYed into the container image and
# executed there. That promise is only true if these stay LF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Not executed as a script, but keeping it LF stops CRLF leaking into RUN
# commands that get baked into the image.
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Read by `just`, whose recipe bodies are handed to bash.
justfile text eol=lf

# The art generators (D-064). `art/build.py` writes generated/manifest.json's
# source_hash by hashing these files' BYTES, and test_art_assets.gd re-hashes
# the same bytes through Godot to prove generated/ is not stale. A CRLF
# substitution changes those bytes, so the two disagree and the test reports
# "generated/ is stale — run `just build-assets`" when nothing is stale at
# all. Rebuilding on a CRLF checkout would bake a CRLF hash in and move the
# failure elsewhere rather than fix it.
*.py text eol=lf

# Generated art (D-064). These are committed so a fresh clone plays without
# installing Blender, and they must never be touched by the line-ending
# machinery — the dev machine has core.autocrlf=true, and a CRLF substitution
# inside a .glb or a .png corrupts it silently rather than loudly.
*.glb binary
*.exr binary
*.png binary

# Authored asset sources (D-20260821-game-assets-are-files). art/source/*.blend
# is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for every unit and building, so a silent CRLF
# substitution inside one does not corrupt a build product that can be
# regenerated — it corrupts the original. Same rule as the line above, higher
# stakes.
*.blend binary

# GUT's bundled fonts. Git's own auto-detection already calls these binary,
# but the catch-all above names them for the first time, and a font is
# exactly the class of file where a CRLF substitution corrupts silently
# rather than loudly — the same reason the .glb/.exr/.png rules exist.
*.ttf binary
*.fnt binary

# The fresh-clone entry point, run by Windows PowerShell before anything
# else in this repo exists. CRLF is the safe side of that bet, and the LF
# the catch-all would otherwise hand it buys nothing on a file nothing but
# PowerShell reads.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
